{
  "dom_id": "step-q112606",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-3x-12.$ Given that $f(-4)=0,$ determine which of the following statements is correct.",
  "worked_solution": "If $f(-4)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-3$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-3x-12}{x+4}={x}^{2}-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-3x-12=(x+4)({x}^{2}-3).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={0}^{2}-4(1)(-3)=12\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the rational numbers is \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-3x-12=(x+4)({x}^{2}-3).\n$$\n\nThe roots of $({x}^{2}-3)$ can be found using the quadratic formula. This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\n\\pm \\sqrt{3},\\,-4\n$$\n\nand the sum of all three roots of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}-4=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the correct statement is \"$f(x)$ has three distinct real roots and their sum is $-4.$\"",
